(702651, 71259, 19780, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition: nice slightly off-center positioning of the setting sun. Good diagonals of the waterline draw the eye to the sunset. <br/> <br/>Camera Work: (Focus/DOF/Exposure)- nice exposure and focus, with good detail on the water surface in the foreground. <br/> <br/>Post Processing: you caught it all in the moment, doesn''t look like it needed anything else.<br/> <br/>Challenge: This fit the challenge, in my view (if you live near the beach). But I would have liked to see something else that gave it more context about the area. At the moment it''s just a lovely sunset, I''d like to see more of what makes this Oregon beach spectacular. <br/> <br/>My Opinion: A lovely photo. Nicely composed but a little on the ordinary side. I would have liked a little more inclusion of the area to help set the scene. Very good quality and you have good eye. Keep it up. <br/> <br/>If these comments have been of help to you, please mark it. If you have any other questions or comments, feel free to PM me.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Composition: nice slightly off-center positioning of the setting sun. Good diagonals of the waterline draw the eye to the sunset.  Camera Work: (Focus/DOF/Exposure)- nice exposure and focus, with good detail on the water surface in the foreground.  Post Processing: you caught it all in the moment, doesn''t look like it needed anything else. Challenge: This fit the challenge, in my view (if you live near the beach). But I would have liked to see something else that gave it more context about the area. At the moment it''s just a lovely sunset, I''d like to see more of what makes this Oregon beach spectacular.  My Opinion: A lovely photo. Nicely composed but a little on the ordinary side. I would have liked a little more inclusion of the area to help set the scene. Very good quality and you have good eye. Keep it up.  If these comments have been of help to you, please mark it. If you have any other questions or comments, feel free to PM me.', '2004-05-06 20:28:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(702671, 72279, 13390, '<td valign="top"><b>*From the Critique Club*</b> <br/> <br/>:D<br/> <br/>Very cool image!<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/>Really, I can''t find any real composition issue, the only thing that really cought my attention as far as this goes  was the foreground, for some reason, my eye keeps getting drawn into the foreground of the shot, distracting me from the beauty of the shot.<br/> <br/><b>Focus:</b> <br/>Overall good focus. I am not usre or not if NeatImage was used, but I personally don''t think it ruined it if you did. It does kind of have a usrreal feal to it, but I think that just adds to the quality of the shot.<br/> <br/><b>Color and Lighting:</b> <br/> <br/>This is what makes this shot and excellent photo instead of a good photo. The lighting is so dynamic. You picked the PERFECT time of day to shoot this. This kind of lighting does quite a few things:<br/>1. Creates different shades and tones of colors, giving you a wide variety from dark all the way through light. Gives the shot a feeling of completeness.<br/>2. Adds shadows that help bring out the contrast and brings out the contours of the land, giving it more depth.<br/>3. Gives you a nice exposure. Nothing is too dark and nothing is blown out.<br/>I would say you hit the nail on thead with the lighting here and that''s why you were rewarded with you''re good score.<br/><b>Other:</b> <br/>A very captivating shot. Makes you feel like you are really there at tee-time. Very dyanamic and very pleasing to look at.<br/> <br/>If you have any comments, questions, or concerns, please feel free to PM me.<br/> <br/>Best of luck to ya in future challenges!<br/> <br/>~Matthew Dykman (goinskiing)</td>', '*From the Critique Club*  :D Very cool image! Composition: Really, I can''t find any real composition issue, the only thing that really cought my attention as far as this goes  was the foreground, for some reason, my eye keeps getting drawn into the foreground of the shot, distracting me from the beauty of the shot. Focus: Overall good focus. I am not usre or not if NeatImage was used, but I personally don''t think it ruined it if you did. It does kind of have a usrreal feal to it, but I think that just adds to the quality of the shot. Color and Lighting:  This is what makes this shot and excellent photo instead of a good photo. The lighting is so dynamic. You picked the PERFECT time of day to shoot this. This kind of lighting does quite a few things:1. Creates different shades and tones of colors, giving you a wide variety from dark all the way through light. Gives the shot a feeling of completeness.2. Adds shadows that help bring out the contrast and brings out the contours of the land, giving it more depth.3. Gives you a nice exposure. Nothing is too dark and nothing is blown out.I would say you hit the nail on thead with the lighting here and that''s why you were rewarded with you''re good score.Other: A very captivating shot. Makes you feel like you are really there at tee-time. Very dyanamic and very pleasing to look at. If you have any comments, questions, or concerns, please feel free to PM me. Best of luck to ya in future challenges! ~Matthew Dykman (goinskiing)', '2004-05-06 20:42:13.000000', NULL, 0),
(703060, 72085, 9668, '<td valign="top">Thanks voters and commenters, I''m quiet happy with the place I got. I also got my highest point score, so that was pretty cool. I hope to score this highly again, without using my beautiful coastline as the subject.</td>', 'Thanks voters and commenters, I''m quiet happy with the place I got. I also got my highest point score, so that was pretty cool. I hope to score this highly again, without using my beautiful coastline as the subject.', '2004-05-06 23:24:02.000000', NULL, 0),
(703120, 72230, 14012, '<td valign="top">**Critique Club**<br/> <br/>Hi Inga!<br/> <br/>This is an excellent shot. IMHO, the tone is just perfect. I apologize that this won''t be much of a long winded critique, but this image is pretty close to perfect, for me. The one change that I think may have improved this would be if only the two posts on the right were there.<br/> <br/>Other than that, perfect!<br/> <br/>Regards!<br/>Chris</td>', '**Critique Club** Hi Inga! This is an excellent shot. IMHO, the tone is just perfect. I apologize that this won''t be much of a long winded critique, but this image is pretty close to perfect, for me. The one change that I think may have improved this would be if only the two posts on the right were there. Other than that, perfect! Regards!Chris', '2004-05-06 23:39:42.000000', NULL, 0),
(703214, 72333, 10402, '<td valign="top"><b>Greetings from the Critique Club!</b> <br/> <br/><b>The message</b> <br/>I think this photo captures some of the character of this old station. The sepia color works well, adding to the "old" feeling. But it lacks an element to grab the viewer''s attention, making it a bit boring.<br/> <br/><b>Creative choices</b> <br/>The face of the station is in shadow, making it appear rather flat. Early morning or late afternoon light (whichever would illuminate this particular side of the building) would bring out the textures of the bricks and cause the metal roof support to cast interesting shadows. I long to see the left edge of the roof; including it would give better balance and hopefully keep the left corner from looking slanted (it isn''t; it just appears that way because of the curve above it). But I like the perspective and the window with the light frame makes a great center of interest.<br/> <br/><b>Technical aspects</b> <br/>The focus is great, although soft (I think a bit more USM would have helped, although that''s really a matter of taste). Being mostly in shadow with a bright sky in the background, the photo is somewhat underexposed, causing detail to be lost in the dark shadows.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!  The message I think this photo captures some of the character of this old station. The sepia color works well, adding to the "old" feeling. But it lacks an element to grab the viewer''s attention, making it a bit boring. Creative choices The face of the station is in shadow, making it appear rather flat. Early morning or late afternoon light (whichever would illuminate this particular side of the building) would bring out the textures of the bricks and cause the metal roof support to cast interesting shadows. I long to see the left edge of the roof; including it would give better balance and hopefully keep the left corner from looking slanted (it isn''t; it just appears that way because of the curve above it). But I like the perspective and the window with the light frame makes a great center of interest. Technical aspects The focus is great, although soft (I think a bit more USM would have helped, although that''s really a matter of taste). Being mostly in shadow with a bright sky in the background, the photo is somewhat underexposed, causing detail to be lost in the dark shadows.', '2004-05-07 00:44:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(703625, 72322, 18722, '<td valign="top">Thanks for the comments.<br/> <br/>I was running out of time on this, as it had been raining all week.  Sure there are a few places in this town that look better wet than dry (rivers look better with water than without ;D ), but all of those were flooded -- way too much rain.<br/> <br/>Just before sunset on the final day I thought of trying this park.  It is newer than the other and there are many who still do not know it is there.  I arrived just as the sun finished sinking below the horizon, and had about 15 minutes to shoot the shots in the after glow of the sunset.<br/> <br/>This was not my first choice of the 14 exposures I took (most my card will hold at max resolution), but partway through the shoot I slipped on a wet metal bridge and jarred my camera on the railing.  This caused it to reset all settings (including the date and time).  Not knowing this at the time, I ended up with a choice between this one and a close-up of one of the geese (seen after they swam away from me in the picture).<br/> <br/>By the time I realized this, I had very little time to prepare the image before submitting it.  Not that having more time would have helped, I overexposed trying to compensate for the very low light levels.  Besides, the only thing worse than my photography skills are my photoshop skills. :P<br/> <br/>David</td>', 'Thanks for the comments. I was running out of time on this, as it had been raining all week.  Sure there are a few places in this town that look better wet than dry (rivers look better with water than without ;D ), but all of those were flooded -- way too much rain. Just before sunset on the final day I thought of trying this park.  It is newer than the other and there are many who still do not know it is there.  I arrived just as the sun finished sinking below the horizon, and had about 15 minutes to shoot the shots in the after glow of the sunset. This was not my first choice of the 14 exposures I took (most my card will hold at max resolution), but partway through the shoot I slipped on a wet metal bridge and jarred my camera on the railing.  This caused it to reset all settings (including the date and time).  Not knowing this at the time, I ended up with a choice between this one and a close-up of one of the geese (seen after they swam away from me in the picture). By the time I realized this, I had very little time to prepare the image before submitting it.  Not that having more time would have helped, I overexposed trying to compensate for the very low light levels.  Besides, the only thing worse than my photography skills are my photoshop skills. :P David', '2004-05-07 07:01:31.000000', NULL, 0),
(703891, 72142, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/>Hello! This is a nice shot overall, well composed and balanced.  But it seems to be missing something, it doesn''t jump off the screen and make me want to go to that park and go relax.  It also seems just a tad tilted to the left making me feel just a bit off balance.  I used my magic envelope and cropped off the bottom on my screen and that made a big difference to start.  Also maybe boosting up the contrast just a bit to really help bring out the colors of the shot and make them jump out so it will grab my attention.  Looking at your portfolio I noticed that a lot of the shots are well nice, I especially like the bearded dragon (I used to have one) but alot of them also looked a bit flat.   <br/> <br/>Also the background in the shot is very busy with the playground and that''s okay, at least it''s not filled with kids to really make the shot too busy :)  But again, with the magic of my trusty envelopes I cropped it a little tighter on the sides, bringing the left to the left edge of the arch and the right side so the bench and trash can was gone. Again, big difference!  I tried taking some off the top but that really hurt the shot, the trees really make this shot, offering a cover and a border at the same time.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps and if you do adjust this shot, I would love to see the new version. <br/> <br/>Good Luck In Future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda<br/>DNeufer@stny.rr.com if you have any questions or want to discuss this further!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club!Hello! This is a nice shot overall, well composed and balanced.  But it seems to be missing something, it doesn''t jump off the screen and make me want to go to that park and go relax.  It also seems just a tad tilted to the left making me feel just a bit off balance.  I used my magic envelope and cropped off the bottom on my screen and that made a big difference to start.  Also maybe boosting up the contrast just a bit to really help bring out the colors of the shot and make them jump out so it will grab my attention.  Looking at your portfolio I noticed that a lot of the shots are well nice, I especially like the bearded dragon (I used to have one) but alot of them also looked a bit flat.    Also the background in the shot is very busy with the playground and that''s okay, at least it''s not filled with kids to really make the shot too busy :)  But again, with the magic of my trusty envelopes I cropped it a little tighter on the sides, bringing the left to the left edge of the arch and the right side so the bench and trash can was gone. Again, big difference!  I tried taking some off the top but that really hurt the shot, the trees really make this shot, offering a cover and a border at the same time. Hope this helps and if you do adjust this shot, I would love to see the new version.  Good Luck In Future Challenges! DeanndaDNeufer@stny.rr.com if you have any questions or want to discuss this further!', '2004-05-07 11:29:55.000000', NULL, 0),
(704489, 72336, 10680, '<td valign="top">This is a great shot, i think you should make a print! :) congrats on your ribbon.</td>', 'This is a great shot, i think you should make a print! :) congrats on your ribbon.', '2004-05-07 16:01:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(704956, 72141, 4540, '<td valign="top">Pekne!!!</td>', 'Pekne!!!', '2004-05-07 22:02:45.000000', NULL, 0),
(704986, 71672, 6900, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This shot does not give me a sense of ''where you live.''  While I don''t think it falls outside of the challenge brief, it''s not nailing me over the head with it either.  You live on the 11th floor?  I like the perspective in this shot, but the flare detracts from the lines/parallels and overall composition.  I see lines, edges and then circles disrupting the flow.<br/> <br/>I like that this is in b/w.  color would be too hard on the eyes looking this way.<br/> <br/>Nice finish!  Happy shooting!<br/> <br/>Mav</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! This shot does not give me a sense of ''where you live.''  While I don''t think it falls outside of the challenge brief, it''s not nailing me over the head with it either.  You live on the 11th floor?  I like the perspective in this shot, but the flare detracts from the lines/parallels and overall composition.  I see lines, edges and then circles disrupting the flow. I like that this is in b/w.  color would be too hard on the eyes looking this way. Nice finish!  Happy shooting! Mav', '2004-05-07 22:49:01.000000', NULL, 0),
(705419, 71957, 13547, '<td valign="top">Wooo Ben u Legend! hehe great snap, now my desktop wallpaper :-)</td>', 'Wooo Ben u Legend! hehe great snap, now my desktop wallpaper :-)', '2004-05-08 08:05:19.000000', NULL, 0),
(705448, 72318, 3585, '<td valign="top">In my critique club incarnation:<br/> <br/>I feel a little uncomfortable with this photo. The setting is a good choice and spectacular but the picture itself is overall a little gloomy, I would much prefer it the other way around. The bad weather could be part of the problem. I also feel a slight lack of enthusiasm shooting a scene already documented several times. There is a lot of distance with your subject(s), the falling waters lack a bit in definition, the rocky face could use a little supplement of saturation, the scene seems a little slanted (even if it is not the case) and the group of people (that really attract the eye) are a little too far to add some human interest to the image. <br/>A tendency we all have when photographing subjects that spectacular is to forget that non photographic elements like the sound and the wind, in this case, will not be represented in the composition if we dont make a special effort in that direction. I think that including, by moving closer, this group of sightseers would have helped delivering a better sense of the atmosphere in this location and take advantage that way of the lousy weather conditions you had.<br/> <br/>All the best,<br/>JJ<br/></td>', 'In my critique club incarnation: I feel a little uncomfortable with this photo. The setting is a good choice and spectacular but the picture itself is overall a little gloomy, I would much prefer it the other way around. The bad weather could be part of the problem. I also feel a slight lack of enthusiasm shooting a scene already documented several times. There is a lot of distance with your subject(s), the falling waters lack a bit in definition, the rocky face could use a little supplement of saturation, the scene seems a little slanted (even if it is not the case) and the group of people (that really attract the eye) are a little too far to add some human interest to the image. A tendency we all have when photographing subjects that spectacular is to forget that non photographic elements like the sound and the wind, in this case, will not be represented in the composition if we dont make a special effort in that direction. I think that including, by moving closer, this group of sightseers would have helped delivering a better sense of the atmosphere in this location and take advantage that way of the lousy weather conditions you had. All the best,JJ', '2004-05-08 09:01:05.000000', NULL, 0),
(707271, 72328, 19780, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club<br/> <br/>Composition: the composition works for me on a color level - I love the color of the sky, how it''s the same as the bars on the fence and then the blues of the water. However, I have not found a successful subject in the photo - I don''t know if it''s the lake itself or the boat or the trees. Perhaps getting closer to the sailboat and taking the same view again would be more successful. <br/> <br/>Background: great sense of perspective and nice sky.<br/> <br/>Camera Work: (Focus/DOF/Exposure) - the focus is just slightly off or maybe it''s a sharpening issue. Only the posts in the foreground seem sharp. <br/> <br/>Post Processing: The exposure on the cement is a little hot, but I don''t mind it. The colors are super-saturated, but I don''t mind that either. I''d hate for you to lose the trio of blues. <br/> <br/>The Challenge: It fits the challenge well. <br/> <br/>My Opinion: It gives a great sense of space and shows a wonderful public area. Without a clear sense of subject though, it leaves me a little less than ethused as a piece of art, but it certainly would make me want to take a walk in that park if I were in the area. <br/> <br/>I hope these comments have been of help to you. Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions about my notes here.</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club Composition: the composition works for me on a color level - I love the color of the sky, how it''s the same as the bars on the fence and then the blues of the water. However, I have not found a successful subject in the photo - I don''t know if it''s the lake itself or the boat or the trees. Perhaps getting closer to the sailboat and taking the same view again would be more successful.  Background: great sense of perspective and nice sky. Camera Work: (Focus/DOF/Exposure) - the focus is just slightly off or maybe it''s a sharpening issue. Only the posts in the foreground seem sharp.  Post Processing: The exposure on the cement is a little hot, but I don''t mind it. The colors are super-saturated, but I don''t mind that either. I''d hate for you to lose the trio of blues.  The Challenge: It fits the challenge well.  My Opinion: It gives a great sense of space and shows a wonderful public area. Without a clear sense of subject though, it leaves me a little less than ethused as a piece of art, but it certainly would make me want to take a walk in that park if I were in the area.  I hope these comments have been of help to you. Please feel free to PM me if you have any questions about my notes here.', '2004-05-09 18:33:36.000000', NULL, 0),
(707450, 72107, 13390, '<td valign="top"><b>*Greetings From the Critique Club!*  :D</b> <br/> <br/>Fantastic image you got here, something to be proud of.<br/> <br/><b>Composition:</b> <br/> <br/>I believe this is by far your strongest element of the photo. The way you have it set up, with the angle and all, makes it look like you carefully considered what elements were needed in the shot and where they should be placed, you have done a nice job here. It is well framed by the plants surrounding the shot and really enhances. Using framing techniques like this can really enhance a shot, especiallly the outdoor shots. Excellent focus as well.<br/> <br/><b>Lighting and Color:</b> <br/> <br/>According to the commenters/slash voters, it appears that this is the most controversial  part of this shot. Sepia, desaturation, and color is one of those arbitrary things when it comes to voting, mostly due to the fact that it is highly subjective to personal taste. However, I can tell you my personal preference as far as this shot goes. I honestly would havbe liked to see this shot in color. I believe the color can tell so much about a place, especially in the "Where You Live Challenge." When I think about this challenge, the word culture pops into my mind, and when I think of culture, I think of diversity and variety, and I think color has a lot to do with that in the shot. The sepia does work, don''t be discopurage dabout that, you did a nice job with that. I am just telling you my PERSONAL preference and trying to give an objective opinion.<br/> <br/><b>Other Thoughts:</b> <br/> <br/>A very nicely composed shot that has some very pleasing elements and enjoyable to look at. Good luck in future challenges and hope to see some more of your work doing well.<br/> <br/>If you have any comments, questions, or concerns, feel free to PM at anytime. I am more than willing to answer an questions you may have.<br/> <br/>~Matthew Dykman (goinskiing)</td>', '*Greetings From the Critique Club!*  :D  Fantastic image you got here, something to be proud of. Composition:  I believe this is by far your strongest element of the photo. The way you have it set up, with the angle and all, makes it look like you carefully considered what elements were needed in the shot and where they should be placed, you have done a nice job here. It is well framed by the plants surrounding the shot and really enhances. Using framing techniques like this can really enhance a shot, especiallly the outdoor shots. Excellent focus as well. Lighting and Color:  According to the commenters/slash voters, it appears that this is the most controversial  part of this shot. Sepia, desaturation, and color is one of those arbitrary things when it comes to voting, mostly due to the fact that it is highly subjective to personal taste. However, I can tell you my personal preference as far as this shot goes. I honestly would havbe liked to see this shot in color. I believe the color can tell so much about a place, especially in the "Where You Live Challenge." When I think about this challenge, the word culture pops into my mind, and when I think of culture, I think of diversity and variety, and I think color has a lot to do with that in the shot. The sepia does work, don''t be discopurage dabout that, you did a nice job with that. I am just telling you my PERSONAL preference and trying to give an objective opinion. Other Thoughts:  A very nicely composed shot that has some very pleasing elements and enjoyable to look at. Good luck in future challenges and hope to see some more of your work doing well. If you have any comments, questions, or concerns, feel free to PM at anytime. I am more than willing to answer an questions you may have. ~Matthew Dykman (goinskiing)', '2004-05-09 21:22:12.000000', NULL, 0),
(707511, 71571, 14986, '<td valign="top">Hi there from the Critique Club.<br/> <br/>I like the colors in this shot.  No, it doesn''t "pop" out at you, but not all photos are meant to do that.   This just has a nice, natural feel to it.  I would have liked a little more emphasis on the main flower section with the water.  I do like the dof although it appears to me that the main subject is a little out of focus.  <br/> <br/>As for meeting the challenge,  I agree with some of the commentors  that this shot doesn''t strike me as having a strong element of where a person lives.  I wouldn''t deduct anything because I can see how it could work - but I wouldn''t add anything to the score because it''s really not a strong part of the photo.  <br/> <br/>I also think that the very slightly faded edge works with this shot.<br/> <br/>Nice job!  I hope you have found this helpful.  I always enjoy seeing your shots and comments in the forums.</td>', 'Hi there from the Critique Club. I like the colors in this shot.  No, it doesn''t "pop" out at you, but not all photos are meant to do that.   This just has a nice, natural feel to it.  I would have liked a little more emphasis on the main flower section with the water.  I do like the dof although it appears to me that the main subject is a little out of focus.   As for meeting the challenge,  I agree with some of the commentors  that this shot doesn''t strike me as having a strong element of where a person lives.  I wouldn''t deduct anything because I can see how it could work - but I wouldn''t add anything to the score because it''s really not a strong part of the photo.   I also think that the very slightly faded edge works with this shot. Nice job!  I hope you have found this helpful.  I always enjoy seeing your shots and comments in the forums.', '2004-05-09 22:12:48.000000', NULL, 0),
(707524, 71715, 14986, '<td valign="top">Hello from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>This is really a nice rural shot, and I think it''s a strong connection to the idea for the challenge.   It''s a nice setting with some interesting features - the silos, hills in the background, the greeness of the area against the white buildings and greenhouses.  They all work well together here, I think.<br/> <br/>I agree with everyone else that the biggest change I would make to this shot is to adjust the rotation to correct the "tilt".   I might also like to see the colors brightened up just a bit, but others may have a different opinion on that.<br/> <br/>Cropping seems to be okay.  I might also crop out a little more on the left hand side to bring the focus more on the main part of your scene.<br/> <br/>It''s an nice entry for this challenge.  I hope this has been helpful!</td>', 'Hello from the Critique Club! This is really a nice rural shot, and I think it''s a strong connection to the idea for the challenge.   It''s a nice setting with some interesting features - the silos, hills in the background, the greeness of the area against the white buildings and greenhouses.  They all work well together here, I think. I agree with everyone else that the biggest change I would make to this shot is to adjust the rotation to correct the "tilt".   I might also like to see the colors brightened up just a bit, but others may have a different opinion on that. Cropping seems to be okay.  I might also crop out a little more on the left hand side to bring the focus more on the main part of your scene. It''s an nice entry for this challenge.  I hope this has been helpful!', '2004-05-09 22:18:18.000000', NULL, 0),
(707827, 72250, 12448, '<td valign="top">Greetings from the Critique Club!<br/> <br/>Hello!  This shot has a lot of potential if done right and sadly, I''m not the one who can tell you the correct method :(  But I will do the best I can with the knowledge I have.  I like the idea behind the shot but the execution of said shot left a lot to be desired.  The lack of focus or details really hurts this shot overall.  Without your description that this is a river, I would have a very hard time telling, I can see very slight reflections in the lower left but I have to really search for them.  <br/> <br/>The blown out lights on the left really pull my eyes over there, making the other "margin" seem unimportant.  I''m thinking if this shot was taken during the day when you could actually see the river.  If you want to try a night shot again, wait for a clear night with a full moon and open the f-stop so more light gets in.  Crop more on the left so the lights don''t distract you overall.<br/> <br/>Hope this helps.  I was looking through your portfolio and love some of the shots you have taken, you have an excellent overall eye.<br/> <br/>Good Luck In Future Challenges!<br/> <br/>Deannda<br/>DNeufer@stny.rr.com if you have any questions or want to discuss this further!</td>', 'Greetings from the Critique Club! Hello!  This shot has a lot of potential if done right and sadly, I''m not the one who can tell you the correct method :(  But I will do the best I can with the knowledge I have.  I like the idea behind the shot but the execution of said shot left a lot to be desired.  The lack of focus or details really hurts this shot overall.  Without your description that this is a river, I would have a very hard time telling, I can see very slight reflections in the lower left but I have to really search for them.   The blown out lights on the left really pull my eyes over there, making the other "margin" seem unimportant.  I''m thinking if this shot was taken during the day when you could actually see the river.  If you want to try a night shot again, wait for a clear night with a full moon and open the f-stop so more light gets in.  Crop more on the left so the lights don''t distract you overall. Hope this helps.  I was looking through your portfolio and love some of the shots you have taken, you have an excellent overall eye. Good Luck In Future Challenges! DeanndaDNeufer@stny.rr.com if you have any questions or want to discuss this further!', '2004-05-10 00:47:20.000000', NULL, 0),
(708329, 71939, 20324, '<td valign="top">Thanx for all the comments. <br/> <br/>I took another photo at the same place but much later at night, so no cars. Does it look better?<br/> <br/>Art<br/> <br/><a href="//www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=75724" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/20324/thumb/75724.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/20324/thumb/75724.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/20324/thumb/75724.jpg"/></a><br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2004-05-10 17:25:34.</i></td>', 'Thanx for all the comments.  I took another photo at the same place but much later at night, so no cars. Does it look better? Art ', '2004-05-10 05:01:20.000000', '2004-05-10 18:25:34.000000', 0),
